---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

const title = "How to read a Dehua quotation | Notes from Dehua";
const description = "A Dehua workshop quotation usually arrives as one bundled number. Here is the four-part structure to ask for, and the four extras that reappear on almost every quote.";
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <Fragment slot="header">
    <Header />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="container article-page">

    <nav class="crumbs">
      <a href="/blog">Notes from Dehua</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <span>Costs</span>
    </nav>

    <article>
      <header class="article-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Costs</p>
        <h1>How to read a Dehua quotation without overpaying</h1>
        <p class="article-meta">November 2026 · about 6 minutes</p>
      </header>

      <p class="lead">
        Almost every quotation that arrives from a workshop on this street arrives the same way: one
        number, one delivery date, one line that says "including mould". It looks tidy. It is also
        impossible to compare with the next workshop, and impossible to argue with later.
      </p>

      <h2>Ask for four lines, not one number</h2>

      <p>A quotation you can actually use separates:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>One-time tooling</strong> — modelling, master model printing, plaster working moulds.</li>
        <li><strong>Per piece</strong> — body, glaze, firing.</li>
        <li><strong>Surface work</strong> — laser engraving, decal, gilding, sanding.</li>
        <li><strong>Freight and packing</strong> — sample freight, bulk packing, tax or export paperwork.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Most workshops quote one bundled figure. The minimum you should insist on is one-time tooling
        separated from per-piece cost. That single split tells you what a second order will cost before
        you have placed the first one, and it tells you whether the quote is front-loaded or back-loaded.
      </p>

      <h2>The four extras that reappear</h2>

      <p>Once you have the four lines, watch for these four items showing up as separate charges later:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Body only.</strong> Glaze and firing invoiced separately from the piece. If you do not
          ask, you will not know until the invoice.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Plaster mould wear.</strong> Each working mould has a finite cycle count. Re-moulding is
          a new charge — cheaper than a new master model, but not free. Ask for the cycle count in writing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Revisions.</strong> Free rounds are usually limited. The limit, and the price of an extra
          round, belong in the quote rather than in a message three weeks later.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Packing upgrades.</strong> Inner tray, printed box, label — priced per unit and easy to
          leave out when you are comparing two quotes that look identical.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Three things to put in writing</h2>

      <p>Before you compare numbers, get these three sentences from every workshop:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>How long the price holds.</strong> A quote without a validity date is an opinion.</li>
        <li><strong>Which processes are inside the number.</strong> Firing, glazing, trimming, polishing.</li>
        <li><strong>Which are extra.</strong> Tooling, engraving, packing, testing, freight.</li>
      </ul>

      <blockquote>
        A number without that sentence is not a quotation. It is a starting position.
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        The reason this matters more in Dehua than in a larger industrial cluster is structural: the
        workshops here are small, specialised and honest about their own process — but they quote the way
        they have always quoted, to buyers who already know the local conventions. You are not one of
        those buyers yet. Asking for the split is not a negotiating trick; it is the only way to compare
        two workshops that are both telling you the truth.
      </p>

      <div class="article-foot">
        <p>
          This note is an excerpt from Part 2.5 of the <em>Sourcing Handbook</em>. The full chapter adds
          the grading language for quotations, the sample-confirmation form, and the four questions to
          ask before a mould fee is paid.
        </p>
        <div class="foot-actions">
          <a class="btn-dark" href="/guidebook">See the Handbook — $39.99</a>
          <a class="btn-plain" href="/blog">More notes</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

  </div>

  <Fragment slot="footer">
    <Footer />
  </Fragment>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article-page {
    --ink: #5c4838;
    --tint: #f8f5f0;
    --line: #d6cdc4;
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem 1rem 3rem;
  }

  .crumbs {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: #8a7462;
    margin-bottom: 1.6rem;
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.45rem;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .crumbs a {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: #8a7462;
  }

  .article-head h1 {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem 0;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .article-meta {
    margin: 0 0 1.4rem 0;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: #8a7462;
    padding-bottom: 1.2rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.78;
    color: #3f3830;
    margin: 0 0 1.2rem 0;
  }

  article h2 {
    font-size: 1.12rem;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 1.8rem 0 0.7rem 0;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  article p {
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    margin: 0 0 1rem 0;
    color: #3f3830;
  }

  article ul,
  article ol {
    margin: 0 0 1.1rem 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    line-height: 1.78;
    color: #3f3830;
  }

  article li {
    margin-bottom: 0.45rem;
  }

  blockquote {
    margin: 1.2rem 0 1.2rem 0;
    padding: 0.2rem 0 0.2rem 1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--ink);
    font-size: 0.96rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: #3f3830;
  }

  .article-foot {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    background: var(--tint);
    padding: 1.3rem 1.3rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--ink);
  }

  .article-foot p {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0 0 1rem 0;
  }

  .foot-actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }

  .btn-dark {
    display: inline-block;
    background: var(--ink);
    color: #ffffff;
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    padding: 0.7rem 1.1rem;
  }

  .btn-plain {
    display: inline-block;
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    padding: 0.7rem 1.1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .article-page {
      padding: 1.5rem 1rem 2.5rem;
    }

    .article-head h1 {
      font-size: 1.4rem;
    }

    article p,
    article ul,
    article ol {
      font-size: 0.9rem;
    }

    .btn-dark,
    .btn-plain {
      width: 100%;
      text-align: center;
    }
  }
</style>
